<body>

<header>
  <span class="eyebrow">Bilingual Presentation · 双语</span>
  <h1>Why Is the Sky Blue?
    <span class="zh">天空为什么是蓝色的？</span>
  </h1>
  <p>Three audiences, two languages — side by side.
     <br>三种听众，两种语言，并排展示。</p>
</header>

<nav id="nav">
  <a href="#toddler">🧸 Toddler · 幼儿</a>
  <a href="#teen">🎓 High School · 高中</a>
  <a href="#phd">🔬 Physics PhD · 物理博士</a>
</nav>

<main>

  <!-- ================= TODDLER ================= -->
  <section id="toddler" class="reveal">
    <div class="section-title">
      <span class="badge">🧸</span>
      <div>For a 3-Year-Old
        <small>写给 3 岁的小朋友</small>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="lang en">
        <h3><span class="dot"></span>English</h3>
        <p>You know how when you splash water, little drops fly everywhere? Sunlight is like a splash, too! When sunlight hits the air above us, it splashes into lots of tiny colors. The air bumps the tiny blue colors and sends them bouncing all over the sky — so when you look up, you see blue everywhere! It's like the sky is a big blue balloon that sunlight filled up. 🎈</p>
      </div>
      <div class="lang zh">
        <h3><span class="dot"></span>中文</h3>
        <p>你知道吗，当你把水泼出去的时候，会有很多小水珠到处飞溅？阳光也像是一下泼出去的水！当阳光照到头顶上的空气时，它就像泼出去一样，散开成许多种小颜色。空气把那些小小的蓝色"碰"了一下，让它们在整个天空里到处弹来弹去——所以当你抬头看的时候，到处都看见蓝色！就像天空是一个被阳光吹得鼓起来的大蓝色气球一样。🎈</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= HIGH SCHOOL ================= -->
  <section id="teen" class="reveal">
    <div class="section-title">
      <span class="badge">🎓</span>
      <div>For a High School Teenager
        <small>写给高中生</small>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="lang en">
        <h3><span class="dot"></span>English</h3>
        <p>The sky is blue because of something called <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong>.</p>
        <p>Sunlight looks white, but it's actually made up of all the colors of the rainbow, each with a different wavelength. Red light has a long wavelength (~700 nm), while blue light has a short wavelength (~450 nm).</p>
        <p>When sunlight enters Earth's atmosphere, it hits the tiny gas molecules (mostly nitrogen and oxygen) in the air. These molecules are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light, so they scatter the light. The key point: <strong>Rayleigh scattering is proportional to 1/λ⁴</strong> — shorter wavelengths are scattered dramatically more than longer ones.</p>
        <p>Since blue light has about half the wavelength of red light, it gets scattered about <strong>(700/450)⁴ ≈ 7× more</strong> than red light. That scattered blue light is what reaches your eyes from every direction in the sky, making it look blue.</p>
        <p><strong>So why isn't the sky purple?</strong> Violet light has an even shorter wavelength and gets scattered even more, but:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The Sun emits less violet light than blue.</li>
          <li>Our eyes are less sensitive to violet, and blue + violet looks blue-ish to us.</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="note"><strong>Bonus: Why are sunsets orange?</strong> At sunset, sunlight passes through much more atmosphere to reach you. Most of the blue light has been scattered out of the direct beam by then, so the direct sunlight that reaches your eyes is dominated by reds and oranges.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="lang zh">
        <h3><span class="dot"></span>中文</h3>
        <p>天空是蓝色的，原因是一种叫做<strong>瑞利散射</strong>（Rayleigh scattering）的现象。</p>
        <p>阳光看起来是白色的，但其实它是由彩虹里所有的颜色组成的，每种颜色都有不同的波长。红光的波长比较长（约 700 纳米），而蓝光的波长比较短（约 450 纳米）。</p>
        <p>当阳光进入地球的大气层时，会撞击空气中的微小气体分子（主要是氮气和氧气）。这些分子比可见光的波长小得多，因此它们会把光散射开。关键点在于：<strong>瑞利散射与 1/λ⁴ 成正比</strong>——波长越短，被散射得就越厉害得多。</p>
        <p>由于蓝光的波长大约是红光的一半，它被散射的强度大约是红光的 <strong>(700/450)⁴ ≈ 7 倍</strong>。这些被散射的蓝光从天空的各个方向进入你的眼睛，于是天空看起来就是蓝色的。</p>
        <p><strong>那么，为什么天空不是紫色的呢？</strong> 紫光的波长比蓝光更短，被散射得更厉害，但是：</p>
        <ul>
          <li>太阳发出的紫光比蓝光少。</li>
          <li>我们的眼睛对紫光的敏感度较低，而且蓝光加在一起在视觉上看起来偏蓝。</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="note"><strong>额外知识：为什么日落是橙色的？</strong> 日落时，阳光要穿过更厚的大气层才能到达你的眼睛。到那时，大部分蓝光都已经从直射光束中被散射掉了，所以进入你眼睛的直射阳光以红色和橙色为主。</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= PHD ================= -->
  <section id="phd" class="reveal">
    <div class="section-title">
      <span class="badge">🔬</span>
      <div>For a Physics PhD
        <small>写给物理学博士</small>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="pair">
      <div class="lang en">
        <h3><span class="dot"></span>English</h3>
        <p>The color of the sky is a consequence of <strong>Rayleigh scattering</strong> in the regime where the scattering particles have size parameter <em>x</em> = 2πR/λ ≪ 1 (R ~ 0.1–0.3 nm for N₂ and O₂; λ ~ 400–700 nm).</p>

        <div class="sub-h">The Scattering Cross-Section</div>
        <p>For a single molecule, the differential scattering cross-section is:</p>
        <div class="formula">
          dσ/dΩ =
          <span class="frac"><span>2π⁵</span><span>λ⁴</span></span>
          [ (n² − 1) / (2πN<sub>A</sub>) ]²
          · ( 1 + cos²θ ) / 2
          · 9 / ( 1 + δ )
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li><em>n</em> — refractive index of the gas</li>
          <li>N<sub>A</sub> — Avogadro's number</li>
          <li><em>θ</em> — scattering angle</li>
          <li><em>δ</em> — depolarization factor (δ ≈ 0.02 for N₂, ≈ 0.03 for O₂)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The angular dependence (1 + cos²θ)/2 reflects the linear polarization properties of Rayleigh scattering, giving the 90°-polarized component.</p>

        <div class="sub-h">Wavelength Dependence</div>
        <p>The λ⁻⁴ dependence follows from the induced dipole: the time-averaged power radiated by an oscillating dipole scales as ω⁴, and the induced polarization scales as the incident field (frequency-independent in the electrostatic limit), giving σ ∝ ω⁴ ∝ λ⁻⁴.</p>
        <p>For the diatomic molecules of air, the relevant polarizability anisotropy is small but nonzero, which is what gives δ ≠ 0.</p>

        <div class="sub-h">Why Blue, Not Violet?</div>
        <p>While the cross-section is larger for shorter wavelengths, the <em>observed</em> blue color arises from the product of three factors:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Spectral radiance of the Sun:</strong> The solar spectrum (roughly a 5800 K blackbody, modified by absorption features) has higher radiance in the blue than the violet.</li>
          <li><strong>Atmospheric extinction:</strong> Ozone (Hartley and Huggins bands) absorbs significantly in the violet/UV, further attenuating short-wavelength transmission.</li>
          <li><strong>CIE color-matching functions:</strong> The human cone sensitivities (S, M, L) integrate the scattered spectrum. The S-cone response to the scattered spectrum peaks in the blue, and the combined S+M+L response is perceived as "sky blue" rather than violet.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="sub-h">Single vs. Multiple Scattering</div>
        <p>For a clear sky at moderate optical depth, single-scattering (the <strong>radiative transfer</strong> limit) dominates the angular distribution. The optical depth τ(λ) at 550 nm is roughly 0.1 for a vertical column, well within the single-scattering regime. However, near the horizon, multiple scattering becomes non-negligible and modifies the angular and spectral distribution — this is part of why the twilight sky takes on different hues and why the full color field requires solving the radiative transfer equation with source and extinction terms (e.g., via Monte Carlo or discrete-ordinal methods).</p>

        <div class="sub-h">Quantitative Comparison</div>
        <p>For N₂ at 550 nm:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>σ ≈ 1.9 × 10⁻³¹ m²</li>
          <li>Column number density ≈ 2 × 10²⁵ m⁻²</li>
          <li>τ ≈ σ × N ≈ 0.04</li>
        </ul>
        <p>At 450 nm: τ ≈ 0.04 × (550/450)⁴ ≈ 0.11</p>
        <p>This modest optical depth is sufficient for single-scattering theory to explain the angular brightness gradient (brighter toward the Sun, dimmer at opposition), consistent with the observed sky radiance profile.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="lang zh">
        <h3><span class="dot"></span>中文</h3>
        <p>天空的颜色是<strong>瑞利散射</strong>的结果，其中散射粒子的尺寸参数 <em>x</em> = 2πR/λ ≪ 1（对于 N₂ 和 O₂，R ~ 0.1–0.3 nm；λ ~ 400–700 nm）。</p>

        <div class="sub-h">散射截面</div>
        <p>对于单个分子，微分散射截面为：</p>
        <div class="formula">
          dσ/dΩ =
          <span class="frac"><span>2π⁵</span><span>λ⁴</span></span>
          [ (n² − 1) / (2πN<sub>A</sub>) ]²
          · ( 1 + cos²θ ) / 2
          · 9 / ( 1 + δ )
        </div>
        <ul>
          <li><em>n</em> — 气体的折射率</li>
          <li>N<sub>A</sub> — 阿伏伽德罗常数</li>
          <li><em>θ</em> — 散射角</li>
          <li><em>δ</em> — 去偏振因子（对于 N₂，δ ≈ 0.02；对于 O₂，≈ 0.03）</li>
        </ul>
        <p>角向依赖关系 (1 + cos²θ)/2 反映了瑞利散射的线偏振特性，给出了 90° 偏振分量。</p>

        <div class="sub-h">波长依赖性</div>
        <p>λ⁻⁴ 依赖关系源于诱导偶极子：一个振荡偶极子辐射的功率的时间平均值按 ω⁴ 缩放，而感应极化率随入射场变化（在静电极限下与频率无关），因此得到 σ ∝ ω⁴ ∝ λ⁻⁴。</p>
        <p>对于空气中的双原子分子，相关的极化率各向异性很小但不为零，这正是 δ ≠ 0 的来源。</p>

        <div class="sub-h">为什么是蓝色，而不是紫色？</div>
        <p>虽然散射截面在更短的波长处更大，但<em>观测到的</em>蓝色是由三个因子的乘积决定的：</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>太阳的光谱辐射率：</strong>太阳光谱（大致是一个 5800 K 的黑体，并经过吸收特征的修正）在蓝光区域的辐射率高于紫光区域。</li>
          <li><strong>大气消光：</strong>臭氧（Hartley 带和 Huggins 带）在紫光/紫外区域有显著吸收，进一步衰减了短波长的透射。</li>
          <li><strong>CIE 色匹配函数：</strong>人眼的锥体细胞敏感度（S、M、L）对散射光谱进行积分。S 锥体细胞对散射光谱的响应在蓝光处达到峰值，而 S+M+L 的联合响应被感知为"天蓝色"而非紫色。</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="sub-h">单次散射与多次散射</div>
        <p>对于中等光学厚度的晴朗天空，单次散射（<strong>辐射传输</strong>极限）主导了角向分布。在 550 nm 处，垂直方向上的光学厚度 τ(λ) 约为 0.1，完全处于单次散射区域内。然而，在接近地平线时，多次散射变得不可忽略，并会改变角向和光谱分布——这正是黄昏天空呈现不同色调的原因之一，也是完整的颜色场需要求解带有源项和消光项的辐射传输方程的原因（例如通过蒙特卡洛或离散角度方法）。</p>

        <div class="sub-h">定量比较</div>
        <p>对于 N₂ 在 550 nm 处：</p>
        <ul>
          <li>σ ≈ 1.9 × 10⁻³¹ m²</li>
          <li>柱面数密度 ≈ 2 × 10²⁵ m⁻²</li>
          <li>τ ≈ σ × N ≈ 0.04</li>
        </ul>
        <p>在 450 nm 处：τ ≈ 0.04 × (550/450)⁴ ≈ 0.11</p>
        <p>这一适度的光学厚度足以让单次散射理论解释角向亮度梯度（朝向太阳方向更亮、对冲方向更暗），与观测到的天空辐射亮度分布一致。</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>

<footer>
  Why Is the Sky Blue? · 天空为什么是蓝色的？ — A bilingual, single-page reference.
</footer>

